body:
### Description

## Summary

PHP 8.5 and PHP 8.6 accept unknown property-hook names when the enclosing declaration is discarded during compilation, even though the same declaration is rejected when retained or compiled normally.

This creates a discrepancy in compile-time validation depending on whether the enclosing expression or declaration survives constant folding.

## Minimal reproducer

```php
<?php
false && static function() {
    return new class {
        public int $x {
            unknown => 1;
        }
    };
};
```

**Actual behavior:**

- PHP 8.5.10: Compilation succeeds.
- PHP 8.6.0RC4: Compilation succeeds.

### Retained declaration control

```php
<?php
$f = static function() {
    return new class {
        public int $x {
            unknown => 1;
        }
    };
};
```

Both PHP 8.5.10 and PHP 8.6.0RC4 reject this during compilation.

The reported diagnostic is:

```text
Fatal error: Unknown hook "unknown" for property class@anonymous::$x, expected "get" or "set"
```

### Ordinary class declaration control

```php
<?php
class C {
    public int $x {
        unknown => 1;
    }
}
```

Both versions reject this with:

```text
Fatal error: Unknown hook "unknown" for property C::$x, expected "get" or "set"
```

### Valid property-hook control

```php
<?php
class C {
    public int $x {
        get => 1;
    }
}
```

Both versions compile successfully.

### Additional discarded-expression example

The following also compiles successfully in both PHP 8.5.10 and PHP 8.6.0RC4:

```php
<?php
const F = true ? 1 : static function() {
    return new class {
        public int $x {
            unknown => 1;
        }
    };
};
```

## Expected behavior / clarification requested

Should PHP reject an unknown property-hook name regardless of whether its enclosing declaration survives compilation?

Specifically, should the discarded closure and conditional-expression examples above fail compilation, or is their acceptance intentional?

If acceptance is intentional, what defines the boundary between declarations that must undergo property-hook validation and declarations eliminated during compilation?

The question concerns compile-time source validity, not runtime evaluation of unreachable branches.

## Affected versions

- PHP 8.5.10 — Windows x64
- PHP 8.6.0RC4 — Windows x64, NTS, VC2022
- PHP 8.6 source commit: `f23b4c222cebe33be93ee8ecaaa14390c5507a08`

The PHP 8.6 executable was built from the specified source commit.

Reproduce by saving each example separately and running:

```powershell
php -n -l example.php
```

## Relevant implementation

- [[Property-hook parser productions](https://github.com/php/php-src/blob/f23b4c222cebe33be93ee8ecaaa14390c5507a08/Zend/zend_language_parser.y#L1160)](https://github.com/php/php-src/blob/f23b4c222cebe33be93ee8ecaaa14390c5507a08/Zend/zend_language_parser.y#L1160)
- [[zend_get_property_hook_kind_from_name](https://github.com/php/php-src/blob/f23b4c222cebe33be93ee8ecaaa14390c5507a08/Zend/zend_compile.c#L9348)](https://github.com/php/php-src/blob/f23b4c222cebe33be93ee8ecaaa14390c5507a08/Zend/zend_compile.c#L9348)
- [[Property-hook compilation](https://github.com/php/php-src/blob/f23b4c222cebe33be93ee8ecaaa14390c5507a08/Zend/zend_compile.c#L9409)](https://github.com/php/php-src/blob/f23b4c222cebe33be93ee8ecaaa14390c5507a08/Zend/zend_compile.c#L9409)

The parser permits hook names represented by `T_STRING`, while compiler validation recognizes `get` and `set`.

The observed acceptance appears to depend on whether compilation visits the enclosing declaration.

I would appreciate clarification on whether this is intended behaviour or a missing validation step for discarded declarations.

